{{smaller block|ous two-ranked branchlets wholly covered by opposite leaves imbricated in four ranks, the marginal ranks larger, acute, and slightly spreading, the others appressed, glandular, and shining. It is cultivated in dwarf varieties as a shrub for lawns, under the name of ''hatchet-leaved arborvitæ.''}}

'''thwack''' (thwak), ''v. t.'' [Also dial. ''twack''; a var. of ''whack'', prob. due in part to confusion with the equiv. ''thack''<sup>2</sup>, and in part to a phonetic interchange, ''wh-'' to ''thw-'', which occurs in the other direction in ''white''<sup>2</sup>, var. of ''thwite'', in ''whittle'', var. of ''thwittle'', in ''whart'', var. of ''thwart''<sup>1</sup>, etc.] 1. To strike with something flat or hard; beat; bang; whack.

{{smaller block|He shall not stay,
We'll ''thwack'' him hence with distaffs.
{{right|''Shak.'', W. T., i. 2. 37.}}}}

{{smaller block|Take all my cushions down, and ''thwack'' them soundly, After my feast of millers.
{{right|''Middleton (and another)'', Mayor of Queenborough, v. 1.}}}}

2†. To ram down; pack.

{{smaller block|The letters he addressed me from time to time, to the number of six hundred, ''thwackt'' with loue and kindnesse.
{{right|''Stanihurst'', Descrip. of Ireland (Holinshed's Chron., I. 42).}}}}

'''thwack''' (thwak), ''n.'' [< ''thwack'', ''v.''] A sharp blow with something flat or hard; a whack; a bang.

{{smaller block|But Talgol first with hardy ''thwack''
Twice bruised his head, and twice his back.
{{right|''S. Butler'', Hudibras, I. ii. 795.}}}}

{{smaller block|Noble captain, lend me a reasonable ''thwack'', for the love of God, with that cane of yours over these poor shoulders.}}

