'''thwacker''' (thwak′ėr), ''n.'' [< ''thwack'' + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] One who or that which thwacks; specifically, a wooden tool used for beating half-dried pantiles into shape. The tiles are then trimmed with a thwacking-knife.

'''thwacking''' (thwak′ing), ''a.'' Thumping; tremendous; great. [Colloq.]

{{smaller block|''Sec. Ser.'' A bonfire, sir?<br>''Sir Ol.'' A ''thwacking'' one, I charge you.
{{right|''Middleton'', Chaste Maid, v. 3.}}}}

'''thwacking-frame''' (thwak′ing-frām), ''n.'' In ''tile-making'', a table with a curved top, on which a half-dried pantile is bent to form by means of blows with a thwacker. ''E. H. Knight.''

'''thwacking-knife''' (thwak′ing-nīf), ''n.'' A knife for trimming pantiles on the thwacking-frame.

'''thwaite<sup>1</sup>''' (thwāt), ''n.'' [Also dial. ''twaite''; < ME. *''thwaite'' (> AF. ''twaite''), < Icel. ''thveit'', f., ''thveiti'', n., a piece or parcel of land, a paddock (common in local names), also a unit of weight, and a small coin, = Norw. ''tveit'', ''tvet'', ''tvedt'', ''tved'', a piece of ground (common in local names), lit. a piece, from the verb seen in AS. ''thwītan'', ME. ''thwiten'', cut, chop: see ''thwite''.] A piece of ground reclaimed and converted to tillage. {{smaller|''Thwaite'' chiefly occurs as the second element in local names, especially in the lake district of the north of England, as in Bassen''thwaite'', Cross''thwaite'', and Stone''thwaite''.}}

'''thwaite<sup>2</sup>''' (thwāt), ''n.'' Same as ''twaite''<sup>2</sup>.

'''thwang†''', ''n.'' A Middle English form of ''thong''.

'''thwarle†''', ''a.'' [ME., perhaps connected with ''twirl'' (D. ''dwarlen''); otherwise possibly an error for ''thwart'', cross: see ''thwart''<sup>1</sup>, ''a.''] Twisted (?);

