{{smaller block|As the dok lasted,<br>Sythen thrawen wyth a thwong a ''thwarle'' knot alofte,<br>Ther mony bellez ful bryȝt of brende golde rungen.
{{right|''Sir Gawayne and the Green Knight'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 194.}}}}

'''thwart'''<sup>1</sup> (thwârt), ''adv.'' and ''prep.'' [< ME. ''thwert'' (as in ''over thwert, thwert over, a thwert, a thirt'', athwart), < Icel. ''thvert'', across (''um-thvert'', across, athwart), = Sw. ''tvärt'', rudely, = Dan. ''tvert'', adv., across, athwart (cf. MD. ''dwers, dwersch, dwars'', D. ''dwars'' = G. ''zwerch'', across); prop. neut. acc. (with the neut. suffix ''-t'' usual in Scand.) of the adj., Icel. ''thverr'', cross, transverse, = Sw. ''tvär-'' = Dan. ''tver-'' = AS. ''thweorh'' (''thweor-''), transverse, perverse, = MD. *''dwer'', *''dwar'', ''dwers, dwersch, dwars'', D. ''dwars'', adj., = OHG. ''dwerah, twerh'', MHG. ''twerch, dwerch'', also ''querch'', G. ''zwerch'' in comp., also without the final guttural, OHG. ''twer'', MHG. ''twer, quer'', G. ''quer'' = LG. ''quer'' (> E. ''queer''<sup>1</sup>), cross, transverse, = Goth. ''thwairhs'', angry (not found in lit. sense 'cross'; cf. E. ''cross''<sup>1</sup>, 'transverse,' also 'angry'); perhaps connected with L. ''torquere'', twist: see ''tort''<sup>1</sup>. Connection with AS. ''thurh'', Goth. ''thairh'', etc., through, is improbable: see ''thorough, through''<sup>1</sup>. Cf. ''athwart.''] '''I.''' ''adv.'' From side to side; across; crosswise; transversely; athwart.

{{smaller block|Yet, whether ''thwart'' or flatly it did lyte,<br>The tempred steele did not into his braynepan byte.
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., VI. vi. 30.}}}}

{{smaller block|The bait was guarded with at least two hundred men, and thirty lying vnder a great tree (that lay ''thwart'' as a barricado). Quoted in ''Capt. John Smith's'' Works, I. 215.}}

readings.c3s1:
¶
II. ''prep.'' 1. Across; athwart.

{{smaller block|And laying ''thwart'' her horse,
In loathly wise like to a carrion corse,
She bore him fast away.
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., III. vii. 43.}}}}

{{smaller block|Cornelius May and one other going ashore with some goods late in a faire euening, such a sudden gust did arise that driue them ''thwart'' the Riuer.
{{right|Quoted in ''Capt. John Smith's'' Works, II. 93.}}}}

2. Opposite to; over against.

{{smaller block|The first of April we weighed anchor in the Downs, and, ''thwart'' Dover, we found our men in ketches ready to come aboard.
{{right|''Sir H. Middleton'', Voyage, p. 2.}}}}

'''thwart'''<sup>1</sup> (thwârt), ''a.'' [< ME. ''thwert'', < ''thwert'', ''adv.''; or < Icel. ''thvert'', neut. adj., after the adv.: see ''thwart''<sup>1</sup>, ''adv.'' The proper mod. form of the adj. would be *''thwar'' (< early ME. ''thweor'', < AS. ''thweor-'', the reduced form in inflection of ''thweorh'') or *''thwarrow'', < AS. ''thweorh''.] 1. Lying or extending across or crosswise; cross; transverse.

{{smaller block|Those streetes that be ''thwart'' are faire and large.
{{right|''Hakluyt's Voyages'', II. 234.}}}}

{{smaller block|The slant lightning, whose ''thwart'' flame, driven down,
Kindles the gummy bark of fir or pine.
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., x. 1075.}}}}

2†. Antithetical.

{{smaller block|It is observable that Solomon's proverbial says are so many select aphorisms, containing, for the most part, a pair of cross and ''thwart'' sentences, handled rather by collation than relation, whose conjunction is disjunctive.
{{right|''Rev. T. Adams'', Works, I. 216.}}}}

3. Perverse; contrary; cross-grained.

{{smaller block|His herte tho wurth ''thwert''. ''Genesis and Exodus'', l. 3099.}}

{{smaller block|If she must teem,
Create her child of spleen, that it may live
And be a ''thwart'' disnatured torment to her!}}

readings.c3s2:
{{smaller block|{{right|''Shak.'', Lear, i. 4. 305.}}
Now he would make that love prevail in the world and become its law; the world, still ''thwart'' and untoward, foils his purpose, and he dies. {{right|''E. Dowden'', Shelley, II. 130.}}}}

'''thwart'''<sup>1</sup> (thwârt), ''n.'' [< ''thwart''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.''] Opposition; defiance.

{{smaller block|A certain discourteous person, who calleth himself the devil, even now, and in ''thwart'' of your fair inclinations, keepeth and detaineth your irradiant frame in hostile thraldom. {{right|''Miss Burney'', Cecilia, ii. 3.}}}}

'''thwart'''<sup>1</sup> (thwârt), ''v.'' [< ME. ''thwerten''; < ''thwart''<sup>1</sup>, ''adv.''] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To pass over or across; cross.

{{smaller block|Pericles<br>Is now again ''thwarting'' the wayward seas.
{{right|''Shak.'', Pericles, iv. 4. 10.}}
Swift as a shooting star<br>In autumn ''thwarts'' the night.
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., iv. 557.}}
In this passage we frequently chang'd our barge, by reason of the bridges ''thwarting'' our course.
{{right|''Evelyn'', Diary, Oct. 5, 1641.}}}}

'''2†.''' To put crosswise, or one across another.

{{smaller block|All knights-templars make such Saltire Cross with their ''thwarted'' legs upon their monuments.
{{right|''Fuller'', Ch. Hist., III. iii. 11.}}}}

'''3†.''' To put in the way; oppose.

{{smaller block|'Gainst which the noble sonne of Telamon<br>Oppos'd himselfe, and, ''thwarting'' his huge shield,<br>Them battell bad. {{right|''Spenser'', Virgil's Gnat, l. 514.}}}}

'''4.''' To cross, as a purpose; contravene; frustrate; baffle.

{{smaller block|''Third Out.'' Have you long sojourned there?<br>''Val.'' Some sixteen months; and longer might have stay'd,<br>If crooked fortune had not ''thwarted'' me.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. G. of V., iv. 1. 22.}}}}

readings.c3s3:
¶
{{smaller block|The proposals of the one never ''thwarted'' the inclinations of the other.
{{right|''South'', Sermons.}}}}

{{smaller block|O ''thwart'' me not, sir Soph, at ev'ry turn,<br>Nor carp at ev'ry flaw you may discern.
{{right|''Cowper'', Conversation, l. 91.}}}}

{{smaller block|"It is no part of the duty of a Christian Prince," added the Abbess, "to ''thwart'' the wishes of a pious soul."
{{right|''Scott'', Quentin Durward, xxxv.}}}}

{{smaller block|No injudicious interference from any quarter ever ''thwarted'' my plans for her [a pupil's] improvement.
{{right|''Charlotte Brontë'', Jane Eyre, xii.}}}}

{{smaller|='''Syn.''' 4. ''Foil, Baffle'', etc. See ''frustrate''.}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' 1. To go crosswise or obliquely. ''Thomson.''—2. To be in opposition; be contrary or perverse; hence, to quarrel; contend.

{{smaller block|''Thwart'' not thou with thy fellow.
{{right|''Babees Book'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 75.}}}}

[Rare in both senses.]

'''thwart'''<sup>2</sup> (thwârt), ''n.'' [Also dial. ''thought;'' prob. a var. of ''thoft''<sup>1</sup> (as, reversely, ''thoft''<sup>2</sup> is a var. of ''thought''<sup>1</sup>), a rower's seat, mixed with ''thwart''<sup>1</sup>, as if lit. a 'crosspiece': see ''thoft''<sup>1</sup>, ''thoft-fellow''.] A seat across a boat on which the oarsman sits. {{smaller|A thwart is usually a special fixture, but a board may be used for the purpose. Some thwarts are contrived to slide backward and forward with the movements of the oarsman, as in light sculls or shells used for rowing exercise or for racing.}}

{{smaller block|Take care of your dress in the mud — one foot on the ''thwarts'' — sit in the middle — that 's it.
{{right|''Whyte Melville'', White Rose, II. vii.}}}}
